---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { resolvePath } from '../lib/paths';
import { Content as DebateEssay } from '../essays/ai-research-debate-paper.md';

// Each entry has two independent halves, side by side:
//   - aiSummary:    fact-based bullets, one line per concrete thing done.
//                    Written by me, not AI-generated, despite the label —
//                    "AI Summary" here means "summary of what was built,"
//                    kept short and objective.
//   - personalNotes: my own reflections for that same session — plain
//                    prose, first person, whatever I want to record
//                    (what was hard, what I'd do differently, open
//                    questions). Fill this in by hand each session.
//
// Add new entries to the top of this list as the project progresses.
const entries = [
  {
    date: '2026-10-06',
    title: 'Fixed missing Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5 models, closed ZO-22, ZO-23 stalled',
    aiSummary: [
      'Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5 were missing from pi even after pi update. The extension was pinned to the npm @beta tag, which resolves to an old 0.5.0-beta0 build.',
      'Removed the @beta pin from ~/.pi/agent/settings.json and updated to mit-parley-pi-extension 0.6.2. The bedrock/claude-opus-5-5 and sonnet-5-5 models now show up.',
      'Closed ZO-22 after the full suite passed (783 tests plus 27 subtests) and all 31 published file hashes matched. Removed 5 stray .DS_Store files.',
      'ZO-23 (stress test) stalled. Each completion prompt used about 14 percent of my context window. The agent said it kept re-deciding the plan and had not written any code.',
      'Changed to four bounded stages, each with a deliverable and a stop condition: freeze scope, implement and test, run the study, review and close.',
      'Asked for a project overview and counts. So far there are 4 shapes, 8 layouts and 9 configurations, and only 5 routes were tried for the 10x10 square.',
      'Asked how to find the best way to assemble 10x10 or any 100-tile shape for joint load and attitude. The answer was a multi-objective search with a time limit, plus a staged joint-load model, which the simulator does not have yet.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `Add your own notes for this session here.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-10-06',
    title: 'ZO-21 comparison and ZO-22 layout search',
    aiSummary: [
      'ZO-21: evaluated all six library layouts under matched assumptions. Ran the four missing full production evaluations and had a reviewer check the comparison.',
      '10x10 results: snake and outward spiral are not dominated. Free-shape results: straight and staircase. Inward spiral is excluded because its peak sampling check failed.',
      'ZO-21 closed with 657 tests plus 27 subtests and a byte-identical rerun of all six cases. No physical winner is claimed.',
      'ZO-22: seeded search with a 64-attempt cap. It stopped after 3 attempts with two new backbite routes. Both are valid and both are beaten by the snake.',
      'Skipping the optional final travel in the snake saves 1.36 s, but its peak attitude is unknown because the sampling check failed even after a tighter rerun.',
      'Timing-only changes gain nothing, since the current schedule already runs at the rail limits.',
      'Each full evaluation took about 558 s. I stopped the first ZO-22 session with /stop and finished the task in a later one.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `Add your own notes for this session here.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-10-05',
    title: 'ZO-15 to ZO-20: design space, metrics, non-snake layouts, evaluator',
    aiSummary: [
      'ZO-15: defined the design space in doc-5. Two cases, a fixed 10x10 square and any connected 100-tile shape. One robot, forward-only placement, and joint loads checked for printed PEEK and for fused joints at 50 percent strength.',
      'Ran ZO-16, 17 and 18 with parallel agents. The first pass of ZO-16 and ZO-17 was incomplete, so I had agents repair them before accepting.',
      'ZO-16: metrics contract in doc-6 and strategy_metrics.py with 82 tests. Unknown limits are not treated as safe or zero.',
      'ZO-17: audit of the 60 N assumption, motor, turning and rail-contact limits (doc-7). Hardware feasibility stays unconfirmed. 244 tests pass.',
      'ZO-18: support for non-snake layouts and reverse travel on installed rails. A full 100-tile L-shaped dynamics run works. 420 tests pass.',
      'ZO-19: library of six layouts, three 10x10 (snake, inward spiral, outward spiral) and three free-shape (straight, elbow, staircase). 448 tests pass.',
      'ZO-20: full-run evaluator in evaluate_strategy.py. Snake and elbow runs pass conservation and convergence checks. 576 tests pass, and peaks that are not well resolved are flagged.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `Add your own notes for this session here.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-10-05',
    title: 'Assumptions sheet, meeting animation, and forward-only turning simulation',
    aiSummary: [
      'Exported 126 assumptions to deliverables/assumptions/ORLO-Zig_Assumptions.xlsx (9 sheets plus cover) with export_assumptions.py. Values are read from params.py, so the sheet matches the code.',
      'Marked z_rail as provisional because doc-1 gives both about 13 mm and a 13 to 23 mm range.',
      'Asked what the q_lB scalar-last label and the extrinsic xyz Euler labels mean. Also checked tile thickness: 10 mm in the inertia model, but the animations draw flat tiles.',
      'ZO-13: cut the comparison animation down for a meeting. Inertial views only, no fixed center of mass star, no center of mass trace, live pitch, yaw and roll.',
      'ZO-14: new simulation where the whole robot turns 90 degrees on each turn tile to keep facing the assembly direction. Chassis and carried tile spin are included, and outputs are in deliverables/animation/forward_only/.',
      'Asked why the force budget is 60 N and whether A1 and A2 differ. It comes from the 0.48 N m motor torque on an 8 mm pinion radius.',
      'Asked if the snake is the best way to assemble. Made 10 Backlog tasks (ZO-15 to ZO-24) and a brainstorming doc (doc-4).',
    ],
    personalNotes: `Add your own notes for this session here.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-10-02',
    title: 'Finished ZO-8 to ZO-12: A1 and A2 scenarios, validation, animation, comparison report',
    aiSummary: [
      'ZO-8 (A1): ten sorties, 1098 rail traverses and 2196 start/stop episodes. ZO-9 (A2): 99 placements, 99 traverses, zero reloads and 18 turn tiles. Two agents built these in parallel, each followed by a read-only review.',
      'A2 mobile mass drops from 134.5 to 85 kg while system mass stays at 135 kg. In-plane center of mass shift is 1.205 m.',
      'ZO-10: validation harness passed 9 gates and 118 checks on full A1 and A2 runs. It also found a 60.75 N force overshoot between planner nodes, which was fixed. 99 tests pass.',
      'ZO-11: A1, A2 and side-by-side comparison animations as MP4 and GIF, with inertial and body views. 106 tests pass and all six media files decode.',
      'ZO-12: seven plots and a comparison report. A2 moves the in-plane center of mass 3.19x more. A1 has 11.09x more start/stop episodes and 5.00x longer rail traverse time.',
      'The report does not pick an overall winner. It corrects some early scoping estimates and lists orbital and flexible-mode effects as untested.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `Add your own notes for this session here.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-10-02',
    title: 'Planned the ORLO-Zig assembly simulation and built ZO-1 to ZO-7',
    aiSummary: [
      'Planned a simulation of a 10x10 tile array assembled in free space with two robot strategies (A1 anchored two-robot, A2 single mobile robot). Wrote the spec (doc-1) and a planning recap (doc-2) in Backlog.',
      'Installed scipy 1.17.1 and ffmpeg 9.0.2 and updated tasks ZO-1, 5, 6, 10 and 11 to use them. Added a 10/50/200 N sweep to ZO-6 to show the magnet force does not change net drift.',
      'ZO-1 and ZO-2: params.py as the single source for physical parameters, and snake_path.py, which generates the 82 straight, 10 left and 8 right tile manifest. Sabotage tests confirmed the import-time checks fail when the path is wrong.',
      'ZO-3 and ZO-4: rail kinematics and a trapezoidal motion planner that shares the 60 N budget between forward and sideways force. Planned rail time is 8.81 min for A1 and 1.76 min for A2.',
      'ZO-5: 6-DOF free-floating momentum core. A saved 3-D run showed center of mass drift of 2.95e-12 m.',
      'Had a Sonnet agent do ZO-1 and a reviewer agent check it. For ZO-6 and ZO-7 I approved the shared physics decisions and two worker agents built placement.py and gantry.py in parallel.',
      '63 tests pass, including a combined gantry and seating run with all 100 tiles and 3.73e-16 m center of mass drift.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `Add your own notes for this session here.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-10-01',
    title: 'Broke the ORLO controls baseline plan into 25 Backlog tasks',
    aiSummary: [
      'Read CONTROLS_BASELINE_PLAN.md in the ORLO* repo and asked the agent to split it into small tasks a smaller model like Sonnet could finish.',
      'Created one Backlog initiative, AA-1, with 25 unassigned subtasks (AA-1.1 to AA-1.25) using only the Backlog CLI.',
      'Each subtask has context, scope, testable acceptance criteria, file references, dependencies and phase labels.',
      'Left out Phases 0 and 1 because the repo and its tests show they are already done.',
      'Grouped the work as requirements and validation (1 to 4), trajectory and timing (5 to 7), baseline controller (8 to 12), scheduling and comparison (13 to 15), sequence, metrics and reporting (16 to 20, 25), and MATLAB and hardware interfaces (21 to 24).',
      'Seven tasks can start independently, including the thermal datum decision, the workbook residual fix and the jerk-limited trajectory generator.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `Add your own notes for this session here.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-10-01',
    title: 'Two subagents debated AI acceleration, then the parent agent wrote the essay',
    attachment: {
      href: '/docs/AI_Research_Debate_Essay.pdf',
      label: 'To Accelerate or to Restrain (PDF)',
    },
    essay: 'debate',
    aiSummary: [
      'Installed the pi-multiagent extension and used it to run two subagents as opposing debaters.',
      'Agent one argued for continued acceleration of AI research; agent two argued for slowing or halting it.',
      'Ran the exchange over six stages: opening cases, claim-by-claim rebuttals, and closings.',
      'Had the parent agent read both transcripts and synthesize them into a single 5 to 6 page essay.',
      'The essay reconstructs five claims per side, the direct exchange, three cruxes, and the common ground.',
      'Noted in the essay that the empirical examples the debaters cited were not independently re-verified.',
      'Saved the output as ai-research-debate-paper.md and a PDF, and published both the full text and the PDF on this page.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `I installed the multi-agent extension and tasked two subagents to be pinned against each other in a "debate" style argument.  One agent argued for the continued acceleration of AI while the other argued for slowing or halting all research.  I had the parent agent take their arguments and produce this 5-6 page essay.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-29',
    title: 'Reproduced Figure 2 from the Astrom and Hagglund PID paper',
    image: {
      src: '/images/fig2_pid_stability_reproduction.png',
      alt: 'Reproduced Figure 2: stability boundaries in the k and ki plane for kd = 0, 5, 10 and 15',
      caption: 'Recreated Figure 2 from the Astrom and Hagglund PID paper: Eq. (8) stability boundaries for G(s) = 1/(s+1)^4 at kd = 0, 5, 10 and 15. Median error against the original raster was 0.40 to 0.53 px over 2251 sampled points.',
    },
    aiSummary: [
      'Located Figure 2 in PID.pdf on page 3, journal page 1165, not page 4 which holds the similar looking Figure 3.',
      'Implemented the Eq. (8) stability boundary for G(s) = 1/(s+1)^4 in scripts/fig2_pid_stability.py.',
      'Recovered the axis ranges from tick pixel positions in the rendered PDF instead of guessing: k from -1 to 10, ki from 0 to 40.',
      'Measured agreement over 2251 sampled points: median error 0.40 to 0.53 px on the three solid curves, 100 percent within 6 px.',
      'Fixed a broken matplotlib install: the default Python 3.11 had an x86_64 build shadowing the arm64 one, so pip3 install --force-reinstall matplotlib was needed before anything would render.',
      'Saved the output, an overlay check against the original raster, and the extracted original to figures/.',
      'Total runtime was under 20 minutes with no crash, in contrast to the two earlier failed attempts.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `Two failed attempts from running out of space.  tried recreating 2 different images with 2 different models, but both failed.  This was likely due to the recursive image gathering function that never concluded.  Installing the screenshot extension helped and the third graph is much simpler.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-29',
    title: 'Figure reproduction attempts from downloaded papers failed on both models',
    aiSummary: [
      'Downloaded the source papers locally and gave the agent the file paths plus the specific figure to recreate.',
      'First attempt used Claude Sonnet on a simple graph: about 30 minutes of runtime and roughly $7 of compute before the session was ended manually.',
      'Second attempt used Claude Opus 5 on a somewhat more complex graph: over 30 minutes of runtime.',
      'The Opus 5 run terminated with \'FATAL ERROR: Reached heap limit Allocation failed - JavaScript heap out of memory\'.',
      'No figure was reproduced in either attempt.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `I prompted for sonnet to reproduce a simple graph and it spent 30 minutes and took up $7 of compute before I ended the session and tried a different (somewhat complex) graph with Opus 5 which also took 30+ minutes and resulted in a FATAL ERROR: Reached heap limit Allocation failed - JavaScript heap out of memory.  I dowloaded the papers, cited its location, and specified the figure I wanted to recreate.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-28',
    title: '3D assembly animation added to the front page',
    aiSummary: [
      'Copied assembly_3d.gif (855x648, 7.3 MB) from the ORLO* report figures into public/images/.',
      'Added the animation to src/pages/index.astro below the intro copy, using resolvePath for the base path.',
      'Wrapped it in a Figure-style block with a caption describing structure orientation during assembly.',
      'Renumbered the trailing data-reveal index so the button row still animates in sequence.',
      'Ran npm run build to verify the static site compiles with the new asset in place.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `I collected all fundamental, physical information about the robotic assembly system including mass, motors, gears, geometries, sizes, etc. and created several python scripts to organize and run physics checks as robotic control systems were built out.  With that, I was able to prompt the agent to build out basic controls for the robots that meet design criteria (speed of assembly, size of assembly, within force specs, etc.).  Afterward, I was able to render a 3D assembly plot that describes the changing orientation of the structure as it is assembled.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-22',
    title: 'Password-protected schedule page and project page rebuilt from the proposal',
    aiSummary: [
      'Pulled real course dates from the syllabus instead of guessing.',
      'Built a 14-week schedule with course and research tracks.',
      'Marked uncertain dates as projected; kept fixed deadlines accurate.',
      'Sized research tasks for agent work with weekly verification steps.',
      'Encrypted the schedule page client-side; verified no plaintext leaks.',
      'Rebuilt the project page from the submitted proposal.',
      'Removed stale "still deciding direction" language from Home and About.',
      'Verified the live site: pages, PDF, and passphrase gate all work.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `I built out an internal schedule and updated the project page based on the submitted proposal.  Opus 5 produced the result.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-22',
    title: 'Built three pi skills for the ORLO* vision project',
    aiSummary: [
      'Scoped three skills against the proposal\'s key deliverables.',
      'Built docking-sim-scenarios to generate reproducible, checksum-verified datasets.',
      'Generated 1944 scenarios in 16 seconds, byte-identical on rerun.',
      'Built vision-model-benchmark to score pose error, latency, and success rate.',
      'Fixed a real rotation-error bug found via an oracle sanity check.',
      'Benchmarked four models; classical baseline underperformed the null model.',
      'Built dev-log-entry, a skill for safely updating this log.',
      'Restored a proposal PDF missing from the working tree.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `I delivered my project proposal as a .txt in my local directory for Claude Opus 5 to read.  I had it come up with three skills that would be usefull as I progress in this project.  It developed docking simulation scenarios, a vision-model benchmark, and an update dev log skill which I am using now.  I still have to explore the skills to learn how to best use them.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-21',
    title: 'The Sept 21 Tragedy: pi loses its hands',
    attachment: {
      href: '/docs/Sept_21_Tragedy.pdf',
      label: 'Sept 21 Tragedy (PDF)',
    },
    aiSummary: [
      'Pi auto-updated and changed how it passes tools to extensions.',
      'The old extension code was incompatible, so tools silently failed.',
      'Fixed the extension to use pi\'s new helper functions.',
      'Verified file reads, writes, and commands now work again.',
      'The bug lives upstream and needs a permanent npm fix.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `I spent 45 minutes tonight developing my own skills, but ran into an issue with pi when I tried to write these to .pi/Agent/Skills.  Pi had lost all ability to utilize its tools.  I had assumed this was the result of irresponsibly executing code in my terminal.  I spent 1.5 hours on my own trying to solve the problem and 2 more hours with my roomate who spends all day in terminal, and neither of us could figure out the issue.  We even executed the forbidden rm rf command to completely get rid of pi and re-install.  It was useless.  It was not until I connected Claude in terminal that we (Claude) were able to diagnose the issue.  I (Claude) provided the summary of the issue and the solution to it on the left.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-16',
    title: 'Assignment: pull session stats from pi session log',
    aiSummary: [
      'Pulled the pi session log from Sept 14.',
      'Counted 4 prompts and 141 tool calls in that session.',
      'Prompt 1: fix the hidden JARVIS easter egg and em dashes.',
      'Prompt 2: reposition the JARVIS tag and fix padding issues.',
      'Prompt 3: push changes and confirm the live site updated.',
      'Prompt 4: fix JARVIS tab still hidden at full zoom.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `I used Claude Sonnet 5 to understand how to execute the assignment.

I then had it pull the session details from the session that used the
most number of tools, because I was curious.

It seemed readjusting sizing and padding took more tool calling than
building the website in the first place... curious.

I remember this session took quite a long time for each prompt to
render a result.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-15',
    title: 'Simplified project scope while direction is finalized',
    aiSummary: [
      'Reduced the Project page to one simple description.',
      'Removed AI-drafted milestones and metrics pending a settled direction.',
      'Removed AI-drafted publication and test-result claims from the site.',
      'Rebuilt the dev log with plain, fact-based bullet points.',
      'Set up a separate, private notes location for personal reflections.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `Add your own notes for this session here.`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-14',
    title: 'Layout and styling fixes',
    aiSummary: [
      'Fixed a bug where the fixed header overlapped page content.',
      'Repositioned the hologram easter-egg element on the home page.',
      'Unified rounded-corner styling and button style across pages.',
      'Removed a placeholder diagram from the Project page.',
      'Removed an inaccurate personal-background detail from the About page.',
    ],
    personalNotes: `When revisiting the website on a different device, I noticed some of the padding and organization on the website was off - struggled for a bit to correct it`,
  },
  {
    date: '2026-09-11',
    title: 'Initial site build',
    aiSummary: [
      'Scaffolded an Astro + Tailwind site with GitHub Pages deploy.',
      'Built the page structure: Home, About, Project, Dev Log.',
      'Added personal photos and a short bio to the About page.',
      'Added a header, footer, and base layout shared across pages.',
      'Added an animated arc-reactor element and hover easter egg to the home page.',
      'Drafted an initial project one-pager and dev log template (later revised — see 2026-09-15).',
    ],
    personalNotes: `Jumped between models to explore cost and performance. Settled on Haiku and Sonnet as Opus was too expensive for the simplicity of the tasks performed. Used a combination of Claude Co-Work and Claude Code.  Used Co-Work to go through previously created files and come up with context that could be fed to Claude Code as it built the website.  I also used Co-Work to explore research directions and help scope something ambitious for this class. I had issues with misunderstanding context when using Haiku.  Switched over to Sonnet, and performance was better. Still have an error with the outlining of some text on the title page.`,
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Dev Log">
  <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 pt-12 pb-16">
    <h1 data-reveal="1">Dev Log</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-base text-space-200" data-reveal="2">
      A plain, fact-based record of what was built or changed each session,
      paired with my own notes on that same work. The left column is the
      objective summary; the right column is mine — written by me, not
      AI-generated.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-12">
      {entries.map((entry, i) => (
        <article
          class="relative border-l-2 border-orbit/30 pl-6"
          data-reveal={String(Math.min(i + 1, 5))}
        >
          <span class="absolute -left-[7px] top-1.5 w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-orbit shadow-orbit-sm"></span>
          <time class="font-mono text-xs text-space-400 tracking-widest">
            {entry.date}
          </time>
          <h3 class="mt-1 mb-4 text-orbit-light">{entry.title}</h3>

          <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-0 rounded-xl border border-orbit/15 overflow-hidden">
            <div class="p-5 border-b md:border-b-0 md:border-r border-orbit/15 bg-space-900/30">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs text-orbit tracking-widest mb-3">SESSION SUMMARY</p>
              <ul class="text-base text-space-300 m-0 space-y-1 list-disc pl-5">
                {entry.aiSummary.map((bullet) => (
                  <li>{bullet}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
              {entry.attachment && (
                <a
                  href={resolvePath(entry.attachment.href)}
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener"
                  class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 mt-4 text-sm text-orbit-light hover:text-orbit underline"
                >
                  ↓ {entry.attachment.label}
                </a>
              )}
            </div>
            <div class="p-5 bg-thruster-500/[0.04]">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs text-thruster-400 tracking-widest mb-3">MY NOTES</p>
              <p class="text-base text-space-300 m-0 whitespace-pre-line">
                {entry.personalNotes}
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          {entry.image && (
            <figure class="mt-5 rounded-xl border border-orbit/15 overflow-hidden bg-space-900/30">
              <img
                src={resolvePath(entry.image.src)}
                alt={entry.image.alt}
                class="w-full h-auto block"
              />
              {entry.image.caption && (
                <figcaption class="text-xs text-space-400 italic px-5 py-3 m-0">
                  {entry.image.caption}
                </figcaption>
              )}
            </figure>
          )}

          {entry.essay === 'debate' && (
            <details class="mt-5 rounded-xl border border-orbit/15 bg-space-900/30 overflow-hidden">
              <summary class="cursor-pointer px-5 py-4 font-mono text-xs text-orbit tracking-widest list-none">
                FULL ESSAY: TO ACCELERATE OR TO RESTRAIN?
              </summary>
              <div class="px-5 pb-6 pt-1 border-t border-orbit/15 essay-body">
                <DebateEssay />
              </div>
            </details>
          )}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="mt-12 text-sm text-space-400 border-t border-orbit/10 pt-6">
      AI session summaries are on the left, and personal reflections are on the right.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style is:global>
  /* Long-form essay rendered inside a dev log entry. */
  .essay-body h1 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .essay-body h2 {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--color-orbit-light);
  }

  .essay-body p {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .essay-body em {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }
</style>
